{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c497","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07476-3862-7671-a25f-75740245e24f"},"sequence":166,"recorded_at":1780531400000327,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"4727530c-d257-449f-9504-a7a1e57e582a","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"4727530c-d257-449f-9504-a7a1e57e582a","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"4727530c-d257-449f-9504-a7a1e57e582a"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"f849b9e7-e92f-4b69-a1e8-172e40aea623"},"task_id":"f849b9e7-e92f-4b69-a1e8-172e40aea623","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"f849b9e7-e92f-4b69-a1e8-172e40aea623","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2z3se0/TASK-260830-2z3se0_review-verdict-rev3.md`.\n1|# TASK-260830-2z3se0 — reviewer verdict, CR-TASK-260830-2z3se0-3 revision 3\n2|\n3|- Verdict: **changes requested** → `to-dev`\n4|- repeat-of: `rev1/F7, rev2/N4` (B4) and `rev1/F2, rev2/B3` (B6). Both are the\n5|  **third consecutive round** on their class. Per the routing rule the next step\n6|  on each is a gate, not a fourth site-by-site pass; the requested changes are\n7|  written that way.\n8|- Reviewed tree: candidate `9b1c084a1b0a970bc2eed187d5155f4191528c51` (base\n9|  `1cb6b93585749df4ef4755ce93e486bcde9e3a6b`), `repository_delta=present`, 29 paths.\n10|- Working-tree OID recomputed against the candidate before the first mutant and\n11|  after every batch, including the three scratch-test probes; unchanged at the\n12|  end (`9b1c084a…`), `git status --short` identical to the handoff.\n13|\n14|## Baseline reproduced by this run\n15|\n16|| Check | Result |\n17|| --- | --- |\n18|| `go build ./...` | exit 0 |\n19|| `go vet ./internal/sessadapter/`, `GOOS=windows go vet` | exit 0 / exit 0 |\n20|| `gofmt -l internal/` | empty |\n21|| `go test ./... -count=1` | 17/17 packages ok |\n22|| `go test ./internal/sessadapter/ -cover` | 81.5% of statements |\n23|| `go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck` | ok, acceptance_cases=88, exit 0 |\n24|\n25|Every gate the rework note claims is reproduced. The producer's numbers are honest.\n26|\n27|## G-A — B3 is answered: the census does the killing, not the floor\n28|\n29|This was the whole point of the round, and the answer is unambiguous. Both\n30|additive shapes were planted with the arm count held **at** 323, so the\n31|inventory floor (`len(arms) < 323`) cannot fire.\n32|\n33|| Probe | Arms derived | Full-suite failures | Verdict |\n34|| --- | ---: | --- | --- |\n35|| M-A1 — 8th package-level ctor `failQuota` + additive arm, direct call | 323 (unchanged) | **exactly one**: `TestRefusalConstructorsMatchProduction` | KILLED by the census |\n36|| M-A2 — inline `axerror.New(...)` refusal + additive arm, no constructor | 323 (unchanged) | **exactly one**: `TestRefusalConstructorsMatchProduction` | KILLED by the census |\n37|\n38|M-A1's message is `axerror.New inside unregistered \"failQuota\" at protocol.go:160:9`;\n39|`TestDerivedRefusalArmsAreAllWitnessed` and `TestWitnessedArmsAreAllDerived` both\n40|logged `323 derived arms across 9 production files` and passed. The count floor\n41|was green in both rows. **The kill comes from `TestRefusalConstructorsMatchProduction`\n42|and from nothing adjacent.** The rev2 kill-inflation shape is genuinely gone, and\n43|the 323/323 figure is no longer propped up by a replaced-arm artefact.\n44|\n45|That closes the question as asked. What it does not close is B6 below.\n46|\n47|## G-B — N4 and N5 are closed, and closed at the right level\n48|\n49|### N4 — `CheckCallBinding` zero-fact exclusions: 5 of 5 killed\n50|\n51|Each mutant keeps the gate and exempts exactly the zero value of the fact it\n52|compares. Each is killed by its own named subtest, driven through\n53|`DecodeManifest` → `Discover` → `CheckRequestBody` → `DecodeTuple` →\n54|`CheckCallBinding`, i.e. the real entry points.\n55|\n56|| Mutant | Killed by |\n57|| --- | --- |\n58|| `role != binding.Role && binding.Role != \"\"` | `…ZeroFactsRefuse/zero_binding_role` |\n59|| `… && binding.ProviderID != \"\"` | `…/zero_binding_provider` |\n60|| `… && binding.AdapterManifestDigest != \"\"` | `…/zero_binding_manifest_digest` |\n61|| `… && binding.ExecutableSHA256 != \"\"` | `…/zero_binding_executable_digest` |\n62|| `… && admitted != (Tuple{})` | `…/zero_admitted_tuple` |\n63|\n64|### N5 — the two false census sentences are now true\n65|\n66|| Mutant | Result |\n67|| --- | --- |\n68|| paired package-level `var a, b = []string{…}, []string{…}`, consulted from production | KILLED by `TestClosedVocabularyTablesAreRegistered` |\n69|| tagless `switch { case … }` planted in `validCapabilityStatus` | KILLED by `TestAllProductionSwitchesAreClassified` |\n70|\n71|`classifySwitch` confines the exemption to `readUTF16Escape` **by function name**,\n72|and `enclosingFunc` returns `\"\"` for a func literal, so a tagless switch inside a\n73|package-level func literal also fails closed. That is stronger than the sentence\n74|promised.\n75|\n76|### The 11-shape census denominator, rebuilt independently\n77|\n78|Reported outside the gate ratio (these are behaviour-neutral plants, not gate\n79|narrowings). Denominator built by enumerating how a closed set can be written in\n80|Go, not by reading the census.\n81|\n82|| Shape | rev2 | rev3 |\n83|| --- | --- | --- |\n84|| package-level slice composite | seen | seen |\n85|| named slice type composite | seen | seen |\n86|| inline `\\|\\|` comparison chain | seen | seen |\n87|| package-level `map[string]struct{}` | seen | seen |\n88|| tagged switch on a non-`operation` identifier | seen | seen |\n89|| tagless switch over the same members | **blind** | **seen** |\n90|| multi-name package-level `var a, b = …, …` | **blind** | **seen** |\n91|| function-local slice literal | blind | blind — **named in the stated bound** |\n92|| `make()` + `init()` population | blind | blind — **named in the stated bound** |\n93|| `const` string + `strings.Contains` | blind | blind — **named in the stated bound** |\n94|| `regexp.MustCompile` alternation | blind | blind — **named in the stated bound** |\n95|\n96|**Vocabularies found over vocabularies that exist: 7 of 11 shapes, and all four\n97|blind spots are declared.** A declared blind spot is a bound, not a defect. N5 is\n98|closed and should not be re-litigated.\n99|\n100|## G-C — the traversal, the survivors, and resurrections\n101|\n102|Gates were found by traversing production source. Every registered vocabulary\n103|table was widened by one planted member; every obligation loop was grown and\n104|shrunk; every numeric bound was moved by one in both directions; every envelope\n105|echo gate was narrowed to exempt the zero value. Each mutant keeps the gate\n106|present. Every mutant was `go vet`-clean, and production was restored and the\n107|tree OID re-verified after every batch.\n108|\n109|**96 narrowing mutants killed of 131 applied (73%); 35 survivors.**\n110|\n111|| Class | Applied | Killed | Survived |\n112|| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n113|| Vocabulary / member tables widened by one (all 63 registered) | 63 | **63** | 0 |\n114|| Obligation loops and capability gates | 13 | 10 | **3** |\n115|| Numeric and string bound guards, both edges | 32 | 12 | **20** |\n116|| Envelope echo gates, zero-value exemption | 10 | 0 | **10** |\n117|| B3 additive-arm probes | 2 | 2 | 0 |\n118|| B3 import-alias probes | 2 | 0 | **2** |\n119|| N4 zero-fact exclusions | 5 | 5 | 0 |\n120|| N5 census-sentence probes | 2 | 2 | 0 |\n121|| B1 defect restored / B2 map widened (verification) | 2 | 2 | 0 |\n122|\n123|The ratio is **lower** than rev2's 91% and that is not a regression: this\n124|traversal reached two classes rev2's did not measure (see B5). Where the two\n125|overlap, this round is strictly better.\n126|\n127|### Round-2 survivors: 11 of 11 closed. Zero resurrections.\n128|\n129|| rev2 survivor | Probe this round | Result |\n130|| --- | --- | --- |\n131|| `successMembers` unpinned | widened by one name | KILLED (`TestClosedMemberSetsAreDerivedFromSpec`) |\n132|| `failureMembers` unpinned | widened by one name | KILLED (same) |\n133|| `manifestMembers` unpinned (+ local shadow) | widened by one name | KILLED (same) |\n134|| `doctorResultMembers` unpinned | widened by one name | KILLED (same) |\n135|| 5 × `CheckCallBinding` zero-fact | zero-value exemptions | KILLED, 5 of 5 |\n136|| 8th constructor, inline `axerror.New` | additive arms, floor held at 323 | KILLED, 2 of 2 |\n137|\n138|**Resurrection sweep: none.** All 63 tables, every obligation loop, all three\n139|8 MiB frame edges, and the six alias spellings are still dead. The area that\n140|changed production behaviour is clean in both directions:\n141|\n142|- restoring the B1 defect (calling the request rule on the success body) reddens\n143|  **`TestValidateSuccessAdmitsEveryMode/archive`** — the archive success is the\n144|  driving case, exactly as asked;\n145|- `checkValidateResult`'s applicable triple and nullable-bool triple both still\n146|  die when shrunk (`…DrivesEveryApplicableCheck/resume_surface_valid`,\n147|  `…/non_boolean_check_member`).\n148|\n149|B1 was fixed at the right level and B2 is genuinely pinned, 4 of 4.\n150|\n151|## Blocking findings\n152|\n153|### B4 (blocking) — the zero-value / absent-fact class is open at 13 more production gates, including every envelope echo gate and the target-write authorization gate\n154|\n155|`repeat-of: rev1/F7 (4 sites, closed), rev2/N4 (5 sites, closed this round)`.\n156|Same class, third round, new sites — and this time on the package's outermost\n157|gate and on the gate its own comment describes as \"unknown targets never write\".\n158|\n159|**Ten envelope echo gates admit the empty string.** protocol.go lines 278, 285,\n160|384, 391, 398, 405, 474, 481, 488, 495. Narrowing any one of them to\n161|`(x != want && x != \"\")` survives the full package suite — **0 of 10 killed.**\n162|\n163|Driven at the production entry, not inferred. Baseline vs the protocol.go:278\n164|narrowing, on the package's own valid doctor frame with `\"protocol\"` replaced by `\"\"`:\n165|\n166|```\n167|baseline : DecodeRequestFrame -> session_adapter_protocol_error:\n168|           request protocol is not the session adapter\n169|mutant   : DecodeRequestFrame -> err=<nil>, Request{Operation:doctor,\n170|           RequestID:0198f4c8-…-1234567890ab, Body:{…}}\n171|```\n172|\n173|A frame that does not name the session adapter protocol at all is **fully\n174|admitted** and returns a valid `Request`. The full suite stays green.\n175|\n176|**Three absent-fact defaults invert silently.**\n177|\n178|| Site | Mutant | Result |\n179|| --- | --- | --- |\n180|| `capabilityMapUsable` probe.go:531 | absent name ⇒ `true` | **SURVIVED** |\n181|| `CapabilityUsable` probe.go:466 | absent name ⇒ `true` | **SURVIVED** |\n182|| `CheckDoctorHealthy` probe.go:673 | `!validCapabilityName(name) && name != \"\"` | **SURVIVED** |\n183|\n184|`capabilityMapUsable` is the one that matters. Driven through\n185|`CheckTargetWriteGates`:\n186|\n187|```\n188|baseline : capabilities without native_read_back -> capability_unavailable:\n189|                target write misses a usable adapter capability\n190|           capabilities without canonical_write AND official_import ->\n191|                capability_unavailable: neither usable\n192|mutant   : both -> err=<nil>\n193|```\n194|\n195|A caller-supplied capability map that is simply **missing** the required\n196|surfaces is admitted for target write. The doc comment states the opposite\n197|(\"An absent name is not usable… which this gate enforces by refusing every\n198|capability it cannot see\"), and the package has no in-repo importers, so no\n199|caller contract makes the branch unreachable.\n200|\n201|The asymmetry is visible inside one test function. `TestCheckTargetWriteGates`\n202|ends with:\n203|\n204|```go\n205|// A missing provider surface is refused, never defaulted.\n206|missing := map[string]ProviderCapability{\"portable_store\": …}\n207|err = CheckTargetWriteGates(probe.Capabilities, missing)\n208|requireRefusal(t, err, …, \"provider:native_resume\", …)\n209|```\n210|\n211|Every **adapter** case in the same function sets the capability to\n212|`{Status: \"conditional\", Enabled: false}` — present but unusable. The absence\n213|rule is proven on the provider half and never on the adapter half of the same gate.\n214|\n215|This is the standard negative shape *absent evidence treated as satisfied*, at\n216|an authorization gate, with the correct test one screen above it.\n217|\n218|### B5 (blocking) — contract string and array upper bounds are not pinned at the edge: 20 of 32 bound mutants survive\n219|\n220|Moving a bound by exactly one admits exactly one member of the reject class.\n221|That is the narrowing shape the DoD asks for, and most of these bounds do not\n222|survive it as evidence.\n223|\n224|Proven at the production entry, not inferred:\n225|\n226|```\n227|baseline : DecodeManifest, environment_version_range = 257 chars (bound 1..256)\n228|           -> session_adapter_protocol_error: … not a non-empty string[1..256]\n229|mutant   : checkStringBounds(…, 1, 257) -> err=<nil>\n230|```\n231|\n232|`go test ./internal/sessadapter/` stays green.\n233|\n234|| Killed (edge is pinned) | Survived (edge is not pinned) |\n235|| --- | --- |\n236|| manifest.go:163 `display_name` 1..128 | manifest.go:178 `environment_version_range` 1..256 |\n237|| probe.go:144 `detail` 0..2048 | context.go:613 `native_session_id`, :636 `opaque_source_ref` (1..512) |\n238|| protocol.go:249/355/445 `MaxFrameBytes` (×3) | context.go:847 `code`, :855 `message`, :865 `remediation`, :971 `native_item_key` |\n239|| operations.go:593 `>7`, :1016 `>65536`, :1046 `>9` | operations.go:933 `argv >128`, :942 word 1..4096, :593 lower edge `len==0` |\n240|| tuple.go:790 `>1024`; lower edges at tuple.go:948, :998, operations.go:933 | tuple.go:96, :594, :737, :822, :830, :846, :981, :1155; :948 `>64`; :998 `>32` |\n241|\n242|The pattern behind it: the arm witnesses use far-out-of-range values (a\n243|2049-character `detail`, a several-hundred-character blob), which fire the arm\n244|wherever the bound sits. `checkStringBounds` is the sole enforcement point for\n245|every one of these maxima, so `+1` is a real widening with no compensating check.\n246|\n247|**This class was inside my rev2 verdict's claimed traversal and was not actually\n248|measured there.** The rev2 121/93 denominator over-reported on bound guards; F6\n249|(the 8 MiB frame edge) was measured and is still closed, the `checkStringBounds`\n250|maxima were not. Recording that so the drop from 91% to 73% is read as a wider\n251|traversal and not as a regression in the work.\n252|\n253|### B6 (blocking, and the reason the next step is a gate) — the constructor census is matched by the package identifier, so an import alias walks straight past it\n254|\n255|The sentence at inventory_test.go:98 and on `TestRefusalConstructorsMatchProduction`\n256|now reads: *\"every `axerror.New` site in production must sit inside one registered\n257|constructor body.\"* `constructorSitesInFile` implements that as\n258|`receiver.Name != \"axerror\"`.\n259|\n260|| Probe | Result |\n261|| --- | --- |\n262|| M-A3 — second aliased import `axe \".../axerror\"` in protocol.go + `axe.New(...)` additive arm | **SURVIVED** the package suite |\n263|| M-A4 — `axe \".../axerror\"` in manifest.go (which imports no axerror today) + `axe.New(...)` additive arm in `DecodeManifest` | **SURVIVED `go test ./... -count=1`, exit 0** |\n264|\n265|M-A4 is the realistic shape: a producer adding a refusal to a file that does not\n266|yet import `axerror` writes one new import line. Under an alias the arm is\n267|invisible to the constructor census *and* to the arm derivation at once, and the\n268|whole repository suite passes. The same hole applies to the other direction —\n269|`var failQuota = func(...) { return axe.New(...) }` at package level produces no\n270|`produced` entry and no violation.\n271|\n272|`repeat-of: rev1/F2, rev2/B3`. Round 1 closed the alias hole in the arm\n273|derivation; round 2 closed the missing production-derived denominator; round 3\n274|leaves the denominator matched by a spelling. **Three rounds, one sentence, each\n275|time narrower.** That is exactly the signal the routing rule describes, and the\n276|fix is not a fourth site patch: the package has **no import census at all** —\n277|nothing pins that `axerror` is imported under its own name in any production\n278|file — so no AST gate in this package that matches a package identifier can be\n279|trusted until one exists.\n280|\n281|## Non-blocking findings\n282|\n283|### N6 — `constructorSitesInFile`'s own comment is ahead of its switch\n284|\n285|The comment says *\"Function-local var-held literals are never constructors, so a\n286|New call inside one always reports.\"* The switch is\n287|\n288|```go\n289|case !initialized: violation\n290|case !registered[best]: violation\n291|case topLevel: produced[best] = filename\n292|```\n293|\n294|A function-local `var failInvalid = func(...){ axerror.New(...) }` is\n295|`initialized`, `registered`, and not `topLevel` — it falls off the end and\n296|reports nothing. It is defended in depth (a new arm would need a witness, and a\n297|changed code would redden `TestEveryArmWitnessRefusesAtTheProductionEntry`), so\n298|it is not a live hole; it is the same prose-ahead-of-the-check habit in the\n299|sentence right below B6's.\n300|\n301|### N7 — the round-3 battery is finding-scoped again\n302|\n303|The rework note's 14 mutants are all killed and its method bound is accurate\n304|(\"battery is scoped to this rework… does not re-traverse the rev2 93-site\n305|surface\"). Stating the bound instead of implying a traversal is the right\n306|instinct and I am counting it in the producer's favour. But rev2's requested\n307|change #6 asked for a ratio **over a traversal of production gate sites**, and\n308|round 3 delivered a finding-scoped ratio for the second consecutive round. All\n309|35 survivors above sit outside the 14. A traversal is what distinguishes 14 of\n310|14 from 96 of 131.\n311|\n312|## What is correct and must not be re-litigated\n313|\n314|- **B1 is fixed at the right level and for the right reason.** The call is gone\n315|  from `checkValidateResult`, the doc comment states why against the pinned §7.8\n316|  success member list, and restoring the defect reddens the archive subtest.\n317|  Resolved by the document, not by a product decision — as it should have been.\n318|- **B2 is closed, 4 of 4.** All four tail maps now die when widened; the\n319|  `manifestMembers` local shadow is gone; the log counts member-map comparisons.\n320|- **B3's additive-arm question is answered.** M-A1 and M-A2 die to\n321|  `TestRefusalConstructorsMatchProduction` alone, with the 323 floor green in\n322|  both rows. The kill-inflation shape that made rev1 and rev2 look proven is\n323|  gone. B6 is a different hole in the same sentence, not a re-run of the same probe.\n324|- **N4 is closed, 5 of 5, at the real entry points.**\n325|- **N5 is closed** and its four remaining blind spots are declared rather than\n326|  hidden; `classifySwitch` names its exemption by function and fails closed for\n327|  func literals.\n328|- **All 63 vocabulary tables are content-pinned** — 63 of 63 killed when widened\n329|  by one member. That is the strongest single piece of evidence in this package\n330|  and it holds up completely.\n331|- **Zero resurrections** across everything I re-measured, including the area\n332|  where production changed.\n333|- **Traceability and README are honest.** `acceptance_cases` 81 → 88 matches\n334|  seven new cases, each naming a real production declaration and real tests; the\n335|  README explicitly keeps the §7.8 clause binding `unevidenced` and states that\n336|  the registry makes no clause-level claim. No unsupported capability is advertised.\n337|\n338|## Requested changes\n339|\n340|1. **B4** — close the class, do not patch thirteen sites. Add a\n341|   zero-and-absent-fact obligation test that derives its subjects from\n342|   production rather than listing them: for every envelope echo comparison and\n343|   every map-presence default, drive the empty/absent value through the exported\n344|   entry point and require a refusal. `TestZeroValueHostFactsRefuse` and\n345|   `TestCheckCallBindingZeroFactsRefuse` are the right shape already — they just\n346|   do not reach `DecodeRequestFrame`, `CheckSuccessEnvelope`,\n347|   `CheckFailureEnvelope`, `CheckTargetWriteGates`, or `CheckDoctorHealthy`.\n348|   At minimum `CheckTargetWriteGates` must gain the adapter-side twin of the\n349|   provider-side \"missing surface is refused, never defaulted\" case.\n350|2. **B5** — pin every `checkStringBounds` maximum and every array cap at the\n351|   edge. The cheapest correct form is a derived one: a table of\n352|   (entry point, member, min, max) driven at `max` (admit) and `max+1` (refuse),\n353|   with the same for `min-1`. A witness that is far out of range proves the arm\n354|   exists, not where the bound sits.\n355|3. **B6** — add the import census the package lacks: every production file that\n356|   imports the `axerror` path must bind it to the name `axerror`, and any other\n357|   binding fails. Then the existing constructor census is sound as written and\n358|   the sentence becomes true. Do not special-case aliases inside\n359|   `constructorSitesInFile`; that is the fourth site patch this class does not need.\n360|4. **N6** — make the switch report the function-local registered-name case, or\n361|   change the comment to what the code does.\n362|5. **Evidence** — report the next battery as a ratio over a traversal of\n363|   production gate sites. The 63-table sweep in this verdict is the model: a\n364|   denominator derived from production, applied exhaustively, with the killer\n365|   named per row.\n366|\n367|## Stop-the-line\n368|\n369|None. Every finding is ordinary rework inside this package, with no external\n370|constraint and no human product, architecture, or approval decision required.\n371|B4 and B5 are bounded, mechanical, and have a correct example already in the\n372|tree; B6 is one new census.\n373|\n374|## Method and integrity\n375|\n376|- 131 gate-narrowing mutants plus 11 census shape probes, each applied by exact\n377|  string or exact line replacement, grep-confirmed present, `go vet`-clean before\n378|  its verdict, and reverted immediately after.\n379|- Three scratch test files were created under `internal/sessadapter/` to drive\n380|  baseline-vs-mutant behaviour at the production entry, and each was deleted in\n381|  the same call.\n382|- Working-tree OID recomputed after every batch and at the end:\n383|  `9b1c084a1b0a970bc2eed187d5155f4191528c51`, equal to the candidate;\n384|  `git status --short` unchanged (6 modified, 1 untracked).\n385|- `go test ./... -count=1` re-run on the restored tree: 17/17 packages ok."}}}
